# core.autocrlf is true in this repository, which means git rewrites line
# endings on checkout for anything it considers text. That is fine for source.
# It is NOT fine for a vendored conformance corpus: a test file's bytes ARE the
# test. html5lib-tests holds cases whose expected output contains a bare \r, and
# WPT holds encoding and parsing tests that assert on exact byte sequences -- a
# corpus rewritten to CRLF measures the checkout instead of the browser, and
# does it silently, as a plausible-looking wall of failures.
#
# -text = never convert, in either direction.
third_party/wpt/** -text
third_party/html5lib-tests/** -text

# Not a line-ending rule, but it belongs beside them because it is the same
# class of mistake: .gitignore has a global `*.png` (build output), and it
# silently swallowed 2,235 corpus files -- the image fixtures that
# Document-contentType, the <img> reflection tests and much of css/ load. A
# corpus missing its fixtures is not a smaller corpus, it is a corpus whose
# tests fail for a reason that is not the browser's fault. The un-ignore lives
# in .gitignore itself (git has no attribute for this); this note is here so
# the next person adding a corpus checks for it.

# The AetherScript diagnostics corpus (unit DG). Same rule, same reason: these
# files are deliberately BROKEN AetherScript and their bytes are the test. A CR
# is a lex error in this language, so a CRLF checkout would turn all forty-odd
# classes into the same "unexpected character" and the gate would fail for a
# reason that is not the compiler's. tests/unit/run-as-diag.sh also strips CR on
# the way in, so neither mechanism is load-bearing alone.
tests/unit/asdiag/** -text
